  "preamble": "The diphthong ei backs and rounds its nucleus to oi. In central and eastern Old French, ei from Latin tonic ē and ĭ in open syllables became oi in the 12th century (rei > roi, meis > mois), while Norman and Anglo-Norman kept ei; the oi later developed to [we] and [wa] (roi, moi, toile). Independently, Swabian (Alemannic German) turned Middle High German ei [ei̯] into [ɔɪ̯] ~ [ɔe̯] (Stein > Schdoi), keeping it distinct from the [əi̯] that comes from MHG ī, so Swabian still separates Laib from Leib and Saite from Seite.",
  "phoneticEffects": "Backing, Rounding",
